Chemotherapy/Radiotherapy-Induced Severe Mucositis and Stomatitis
Haematology & Oncology
Exquisitely tender erythematous oral mucosa, Painful coalescing ulcerations, Severe odynophagia/inability to eat, Onset 5-14 days post-treatment

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-012
Reason for Visit: Unmanageable oral pain and absolute inability to swallow oral fluids requiring urgent pain management and hydration assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient 10 days post-chemotherapy presenting with sudden, severe odynophagia and exquisitely painful oral ulcers preventing any fluid intake for the past 14 hours. Urgent physical evaluation was required to assess for impending dehydration, secondary systemic infection, and to prescribe rapidly acting mucosal analgesia, which could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My mouth feels like it is on fire, I have these huge white sores everywhere, and I cannot even swallow my own spit without crying."
History of Present Illness: The patient, currently 10 days post-initiation of their chemotherapy cycle, reports an insidious onset of an erythematous, sore mouth starting three days ago. This rapidly progressed over the last 24 hours to the development of deep, exquisitely tender coalescing ulcerations across the buccal mucosa and tongue. They report severe odynophagia leading to a complete inability to tolerate solid foods or liquids since this morning. They complain of thick, ropy saliva, significant generalized fatigue, and dysarthria due to the intense pain when moving their tongue.
Functional Status: Independent mobility, but functionally debilitated by severe pain and lack of caloric intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of rapid dehydration and malnourishment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Distressed adult, drooling slightly into a tissue, speaking in a muffled, hesitant voice.
Physical Exam:
Head/Neck: Oral cavity reveals intensely erythematous, swollen mucosa. Multiple deep, painful, coalescing ulcerations covered by a yellow-white fibrinous pseudomembrane are visible on the lateral tongue, buccal mucosa, and soft palate. Minimal spontaneous bleeding noted upon tongue depression.
Gastrointestinal: Thick, ropy saliva present; pronounced xerostomia. Normal swallow reflex, but severely limited by pain.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Capillary refill 2 seconds.
Lymphatic: Mild, tender submandibular lymphadenopathy bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Multiple untouched glasses of water and nutritional supplements on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter paracetamol with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chemotherapy/Radiotherapy-Induced Severe Mucositis and Stomatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Oral Candidiasis (Thrush) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the presence of deep, crater-like ulcerations rather than superficial, easily scraped white plaques, though candidiasis can be a secondary complication.

Primary Herpes Simplex Virus Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the distinct 10-day post-chemotherapy timeline and the diffuse, coalescing nature of the fibrinous ulcers rather than clustered vesicles.

Stevens-Johnson Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of targetoid skin lesions, lack of conjunctival involvement, and classic temporal onset correlating purely with antineoplastic therapy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits severe, grade 3-4 WHO toxicity criteria oral mucositis, directly resulting from recent chemotherapy. The exquisite tenderness and severe odynophagia have caused a total cessation of oral intake, leading to early clinical dehydration (tachycardia, thick saliva) and significant distress. Delaying assessment until daytime hours would result in severe volume depletion, potential acute kidney injury, and unnecessary prolonged agony. This presentation strictly meets the criteria for an urgent after-hours home visit to institute potent topical and systemic analgesia and establish an immediate hydration plan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed viscous lidocaine 2 percent oral topical solution to be used before attempts at hydration. Prescribed oral liquid oxycodone 5mg PRN for breakthrough systemic pain. Arranged urgent outpatient follow-up for potential intravenous hydration if oral intake fails.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Apply the numbing mouthwash exactly 15 minutes before attempting to drink fluids. By predictably blocking the local pain receptors immediately prior to swallowing, you enable yourself to consume necessary hydration without the severe reflex pain.

Switch to consuming only cold, smooth, non-acidic liquids like milkshakes or liquid meal replacements. By eliminating mechanical friction and chemical irritation, you prevent further trauma to the exposed, fragile mucosal nerve endings.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Soft-bristled toothbrush recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24 hours to confirm fluid intake has resumed; otherwise, ED admission for IV fluids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a fever over 38.0 C, if you cannot swallow any fluids despite the numbing medication by morning, or if you begin vomiting blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This severe inflammation is a recognized consequence of your specific chemotherapy, and the primary risk is dehydration from the inability to swallow.
Clinical Course: The ulcers will take several days to heal as your immune system recovers. The immediate goal is strictly managing the pain to allow you to drink.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must monitor your temperature tonight. An infection on top of these ulcers can spread rapidly. If a fever spikes, proceed to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chemotherapy has damaged the rapidly dividing cells lining your mouth. The viscous liquid will temporarily numb the exposed nerves so you can swallow, while the strong painkiller will manage the deep, radiating ache.
Actionable Steps: Use the numbing wash strictly before drinking. Avoid hot, spicy, or rough foods entirely.

